WEBVTT

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.480
Course structure is the path learners follow through the experience.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.925
Menus, sections, lessons, slides, blocks,

00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:09.200
and navigation patterns should help learners understand where they are and what comes

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:09.560
next.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.880
A good structure creates a sense of progress.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.940
It also makes the course easier to maintain because related content is grouped logically

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.815
instead of scattered across disconnected screens.

00:00:18.815 --> 00:00:22.240
Learning flow should move from context to explanation to example to practice or

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.000
reflection.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.750
Not every module needs all of those pieces,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.740
but learners usually benefit from knowing why a topic matters before they are asked to

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.876
remember details.

00:00:29.876 --> 00:00:31.620
Progressive disclosure is useful here.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.130
Instead of showing everything at once,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:36.130
reveal information in manageable layers when the learner is ready for it.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.680
Screen layout matters because it shapes attention.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.935
Visual hierarchy tells learners what to read first, what supports the main point,

00:00:41.935 --> 00:00:43.400
and what action to take.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.220
Use headings, spacing, contrast, grouping, and consistent patterns to guide the eye.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.000
A screen should not make every element compete for attention.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.775
When everything is emphasized, nothing is emphasized.

00:00:52.775 --> 00:00:54.100
Avoid overcrowded screens.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.820
Dense paragraphs, too many icons, excessive motion,

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.135
and unnecessary decorative elements can increase cognitive load.

00:01:00.135 --> 00:01:03.920
A strong screen supports learning by making the main idea easier to process.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.720
Decoration is only useful when it clarifies meaning, sets context, or improves navigation.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.445
The goal is not to fill a canvas; the goal is to make the learning task easier.

00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:16.220
One practical screen-design test is to ask what the learner should notice within the first

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.210
few seconds.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.830
If the answer is unclear, the screen may need a stronger heading,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.120
fewer competing elements, or a simpler layout.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.760
The best screens feel calm because the designer has already decided what matters most.
